number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|A foreign-domain principal administers a hybrid bridge|A principal from another domain or forest has effective administrative access to a synchronization or federation host through a verified trust and authorization chain.|Trust direction/type; selective authentication; SID filtering; group scope and nesting; local rights; host-to-tenant binding.|Remove unintended cross-forest administrative grants and restrict authentication to necessary resources.|Trust existence alone grants no host administration. Selective authentication, local deny rights, and group scope can block the path.|T1078.002|AC-3;AC-4;AC-20|Constrain the authentication and authorization that cross an external administrative boundary.||High
002|SID history preserves unintended access to a hybrid control-plane asset|A principal carries a historical SID that is accepted across the relevant trust AND that SID contributes effective administrative access to a confirmed bridge host.|sIDHistory metadata; historical SID resolution; trust filtering; effective target access; migration approval.|Remove obsolete historical access after validating migration dependencies and repair filtering where appropriate.|SID history is not automatically malicious. Filtered SIDs or approved time-bound migrations do not establish unintended effective access.|T1134.005|AC-3;AC-6;IA-4|Maintain identifier integrity and remove historical identifiers that retain excessive access.||High
003|A trust-management ACL can open a route to hybrid assets|An unapproved principal can modify a trust object's security-relevant attributes AND a supported change would affect authentication to a domain hosting hybrid control-plane assets.|Trust-object effective ACL; writable attributes; trust direction; target domain assets; current authentication restrictions.|Restrict trust-object changes and independently review cross-boundary administration.|Do not turn any attribute write into full trust control or assume a change can defeat SID filtering without proving the supported mechanism.|T1484.002|AC-4;CM-3;CM-5|Protect changes to inter-domain authentication and authorization relationships.||High
004|External authentication claims protect a sensitive guest grant without adequate assurance|An external identity has a sensitive resource grant AND the resource tenant trusts home-tenant MFA or device claims AND an approved home-tenant assessment identifies an applicable assurance gap.|Guest object and home tenant ID; resource grant; cross-tenant trust settings; current external assessment; effective resource policy.|Review external trust scope and require sufficient authentication assurance for the resource.|Unknown home-tenant posture is Unknown, not an automatic failure. B2B trust is not evidence of compromise.|T1078.004|AC-20;IA-8;AC-3|Control external access and the assurance accepted from another identity provider.|MS-CA|High
005|A controllable home identity holds privileged guest access elsewhere|A verified on-premises control path reaches a home-tenant user AND that exact external identity has active privileged access in another assessed tenant.|Home identity mapping; guest external identity IDs; resource tenant consent and grants; collection scope for both tenants.|Break the source control path and review privileged guest assignments.|Matching mail addresses are not enough. Without authorized evidence from the home tenant, the full path remains Unknown.|T1098;T1078.004|AC-6;AC-20;IA-8|Limit privilege carried across an external identity relationship.||High
006|An AD-controlled cloud principal can weaken cross-tenant access policy|A principal controlled through an AD path has effective permission to change cross-tenant trust settings AND the policy governs sensitive external grants.|Source path; active cloud permissions; cross-tenant policy scope; affected resource grants.|Separate cross-tenant policy administration and constrain privileged changes.|A role assignment outside the relevant policy scope does not establish modification authority.|T1484.002|AC-3;AC-4;CM-5|Restrict changes to external trust and information-flow controls.|MS-APP-PERMS|High
007|Writable DNS records influence a hybrid authentication endpoint|An unapproved principal can modify a DNS record used by an active hybrid authentication dependency AND effective endpoint validation or transport protection would not reject redirection.|DNS zone and record ACL; verified dependency; client resolution path; TLS/authentication validation configuration; network scope.|Restrict record updates and enforce endpoint identity and protected transport.|DNS write access alone is not credential theft. Correct certificate validation and authenticated protocols can prevent useful redirection.|T1557|AC-3;SC-8;SC-23|Protect the name-to-endpoint binding and authenticated communication across the hybrid path.|MS-LDAP|High
008|Application Proxy access reaches a broadly delegated internal service|An accessible cloud-published application uses a connector with effective Kerberos delegation to a sensitive on-premises service AND connector control or access policy violates the approved boundary.|Application assignment; preauthentication and CA policy; connector host control; exact KCD SPNs; service authorization.|Narrow application access, protect the connector, and constrain delegation to the intended service.|A published app is not arbitrary network access. Protocol transition, SPN mapping, and service authorization must all be considered.|T1550.003;T1078.004|AC-3;AC-6;SC-7|Constrain the combination of external access and internal delegated authentication.|MS-APP-PROXY|High
